.so man.macros
.TH ck::pty n 1.0 Ck "Ck Built-In Commands"
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
ck::pty \- Allocate a pty pair for testing or external Ck sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBck::pty open\fR ?\fB-rows\fR \fIN\fR? ?\fB-cols\fR \fIN\fR?
.BE

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBck::pty\fR exposes ncurses-friendly pty primitives at the Tcl level.
It is intended primarily for test harnesses that want to drive a Ck
session against a controlled pty so they can inject synthetic
keystrokes, snapshot the rendered output, and assert against widget
behaviour without taking over the test process's own controlling
terminal.

.SS \fBck::pty open\fR ?\fB-rows\fR \fIN\fR? ?\fB-cols\fR \fIN\fR?
.PP
Allocates a pseudoterminal pair via \fBopenpty\fR(3) and returns a
two-element list \fB{\fImasterChan\fB \fIslaveChan\fB}\fR.  Both ends
are wrapped in Tcl channels and registered with the calling
interpreter, so they can be passed directly to \fBchan read\fR,
\fBchan puts\fR, and friends, and they participate in normal channel
cleanup on \fBchan close\fR.
.PP
Channel options applied at creation time:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
\fIMaster:\fR \fB-translation\ binary\fR, \fB-buffering\ none\fR,
\fB-blocking\ 0\fR.  Non-blocking and unbuffered makes it cheap for
tests to do "drain whatever's available" reads without hanging when
the slave hasn't written yet.
.IP \(bu 4
\fISlave:\fR \fB-translation\ binary\fR, \fB-buffering\ none\fR.
Blocking remains on by default so that injected keystrokes don't
silently get short-written.
.RE
.PP
The slave channel can be passed straight to \fBck::open\ -pty\fR; the
underlying fd is extracted via \fBTcl_GetChannelHandle\fR.
\fIfd\fR-form integers are also accepted for the same option, so a
test that already has a raw fd in hand doesn't need to wrap it.
.PP
The optional \fB-rows\fR and \fB-cols\fR pair, when both supplied with
positive values, sets the pty's window size (\fBTIOCSWINSZ\fR) before
\fBck::pty open\fR returns.  Either both must be set or neither;
supplying just one is a Tcl error.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A test that creates a button, sends Return to it, and checks that the
\fB-command\fR fired:
.CS
set ::ck_no_auto_open 1
package require ck

lassign [\fBck::pty open\fR \-rows 24 \-cols 80] master slave
\fBck::open\fR \-pty $slave .

button .b \-text Hello \-command {set ::pressed 1}
pack .b
focus .b
update                         ;\fI# initial paint\fR

puts \-nonewline $master "\\r"   ;\fI# inject Return\fR
flush $master
update                         ;\fI# dispatch\fR

if {$::pressed} {
    puts "ok"
}

destroy .
chan close $master
chan close $slave
.CE

.SH NOTES
.PP
The slave end is intentionally returned as a Tcl channel rather than
a raw fd so the test process can clean up uniformly with
\fBchan close\fR.  When the slave channel is passed to
\fBck::open\ -pty\fR, ncurses dups its fd internally for use by
\fBnewterm\fR(3); the original Tcl channel remains valid until the
test closes it.

.SH SEE ALSO
ck::open(n), destroy(n)

.SH KEYWORDS
pty, openpty, test, channel
